winex11.drv: glFinish() before reading back an offscreen swap with no OML wait

Word's licensing dialog (and, per docs/office365-under-wine.md, at least one
other window) presents through client_surface_present -- redirect the
GL-rendered content offscreen, XGetImage its pixels, NtGdiStretchBlt them into
the real toplevel -- from a different thread than the one that just rendered
it. x11drv_surface_swap already knows this needs synchronization: when
GLX_OML_sync_control is available it calls glXSwapBuffersMscOML then
glXWaitForSbcOML, which genuinely waits for the swap. When it is not -- true
on both an Xvfb test display and a real Mutter/Xwayland desktop, measured on
this tree -- the fallback was a bare XFlush(), which flushes the X11 protocol
queue and nothing else. It does not wait for the GPU or software renderer to
finish the draw commands the swap just submitted, and client_surface_present
runs immediately after regardless.

Measured with patch 0009's diagnostics: the same dialog, redrawn fresh before
every single present (a real ClearRenderTargetView plus two
DrawIndexedInstanced calls each time, not a skipped frame), read back
alternating between the correct content and solid black, present to present,
purely depending on which side of the race won that time. A one-line addition
to wined3d/swapchain.c first measured swapchain->device->context_count == 1
for this dialog's device, which explains why swapchain_gl_present's own
glFinish() guard (gated on context_count > 1, there for a different reason --
AirSpace's cross-device compositing, patch 0008) never covers it either; that
measurement is kept as a diagnostic even though the swap for this present
turned out to go through x11drv_surface_swap rather than that function.

The fix is the same one the sibling .flush callbacks two screens down in the
same file already use for the same reason (x11drv_surface_flush,
x11drv_egl_surface_flush): glFinish() before the fallback XFlush(), so the
CPU-side call actually blocks until the GPU is done, not just until the X11
request reached the server.

Confirmed on the exact same dialog, exact same xwd -id read that returned
solid black every time before this: 60546 white pixels, Word's blue, and
every line of the dialog's text, legible. Not re-confirmed on the real
desktop this session -- the safe-mode dialog gating that specific run landed
off-window and neither a coordinate click nor a keyboard accelerator reached
it, unrelated to this fix -- see docs/office365-under-wine.md for what was
and was not re-measured there.

diff --git a/dlls/wined3d/swapchain.c b/dlls/wined3d/swapchain.c
index a70832c..38c48c3 100644
--- a/dlls/wined3d/swapchain.c
+++ b/dlls/wined3d/swapchain.c
@@ -647,6 +647,11 @@ static void swapchain_gl_present(struct wined3d_swapchain *swapchain,
 
         swapchain_blit(swapchain, context, src_rect, dst_rect);
 
+        /* altars: measurement only -- is context_count == 1 really why this
+         * swapchain's present never forces the glFinish() below? */
+        TRACE("altars-context-count hwnd %p context_count %u.\n",
+                swapchain->state.device_window, swapchain->device->context_count);
+
         if (swapchain->device->context_count > 1)
         {
             WARN_(d3d_perf)("Multiple contexts, calling glFinish() to enforce ordering.\n");
diff --git a/dlls/winex11.drv/opengl.c b/dlls/winex11.drv/opengl.c
index 01eb3b9..97d2213 100644
--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/opengl.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/opengl.c
@@ -1478,7 +1478,22 @@ static BOOL x11drv_surface_swap( struct opengl_drawable *base )
         if (pglXWaitForSbcOML) pglXWaitForSbcOML( gdi_display, gl->drawable, target_sbc, &ust, &msc, &sbc );
     }
 
-    if (offscreen && !pglXWaitForSbcOML) XFlush( gdi_display );
+    if (offscreen && !pglXWaitForSbcOML)
+    {
+        /* altars: no GLX_OML_sync_control support (common on a software or
+         * virtual GL renderer) means nothing above actually waited for this
+         * swap's rendering to complete -- XFlush alone only flushes the X11
+         * protocol stream, not the GPU/renderer, and client_surface_present
+         * reads this drawable's pixels from a different thread right after
+         * this returns. Measured as a real race (patches/office/0009,
+         * "the missing measurement, taken" in docs/office365-under-wine.md):
+         * the same content is drawn every time and read back as black on
+         * roughly half of presents. glFinish() is the actual wait; XFlush
+         * stays for the X-side request itself, same order as the sibling
+         * .flush callbacks below already use for the same reason. */
+        funcs->p_glFinish();
+        XFlush( gdi_display );
+    }
 
     client_surface_present( base->client );
     return TRUE;
